The oldest story of Saint Nicholas has no Santa Claus and no bag of gold. It has a bishop of Myra running to an execution ground, taking the sword from the executioner's hands, and telling the governor that the real criminal was a bribe. Almost nothing about him can be proved, yet the Orthodox Church honors him above nearly every saint, and the West calls him the patron of sailors and children. How did a man history barely recorded become the saint whom half of Christendom calls on in trouble? Every layer of his story, from the oldest to the latest, shows the same man.